.TH CTPOOL_FUTURE_FREE 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_future_free \- release the caller's reference to a future
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

void ctpool_future_free(ctpool_future *f);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Takes one off the reference count of the future. The library frees the
future object with the standard free() when that count reaches zero.
Three kinds of holder release a reference: the caller, the worker, and
every ctpool_future_get call that is still in progress. For a detached
future the producer holds one in place of the worker.

This never touches the void* result value of the task function. This
module never allocates, copies or frees that value. See
ctpool_future_get(3) for who owns the result.

Call this exactly one time for each ctpool_submit_future,
ctpool_try_submit_future, ctpool_timed_submit_future or
ctpool_future_create_detached call that succeeds. Do so whether or not
anything called ctpool_future_get.

A call with no ctpool_future_get at all gives fire-and-forget behavior.
The task still runs. The library frees the future by itself when the
worker finishes, and it discards the result.

You can call this while other threads are inside ctpool_future_get on
the same future. Each of them holds a reference of its own. The library
therefore destroys the future only after the last of them leaves.

This call ends the claim of the caller on f, in the same way as when a
thread gives up its last reference. The caller must not use f again
after this call. The caller must also not hand f to a thread that is
not yet inside ctpool_future_get.

A call to ctpool_future_free with a NULL pointer does nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
f   Future handle
.fi
